  <article class="prose max-w-none">
    <p>
      If you have ever tried to lose weight, you have likely encountered the term"starvation mode." The common narrative warns that if you cut your calories too low, your body will panic, hold onto fat, and completely halt your weight loss—or worse, cause you to magically gain weight out of thin air. It is often presented as a terrifying biological switch that flips the moment your calorie intake drops below a certain threshold.
    </p>
    <p>
      But does human biology actually work this way? The short answer is unequivocally no."Starvation mode," as it is popularly understood in fitness magazines and online forums, is a myth. However, the phenomenon people are actually experiencing when they stop losing weight is a very real, scientifically proven, and well-documented process known as <strong>metabolic adaptation</strong> (sometimes referred to clinically as adaptive thermogenesis).
    </p>
    <p>
      Understanding the difference between these two concepts is the key to overcoming weight loss plateaus, building a sustainable diet plan, and achieving your long-term fitness goals without feeling like your own body is working against you.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-brand-soft border-l-4 border-brand p-4 my-8 rounded-r-lg">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-0">Ready to calculate your optimal deficit?</h3>
      <p class="text-ink mb-4">
        Stop guessing and start tracking. Use our science-based calculator to find the perfect calorie target for healthy, sustainable weight loss without triggering excessive metabolic adaptation.
      </p>
      <a href="/calorie-deficit-calculator/" class="inline-block bg-brand text-white font-bold py-2 px-6 rounded hover:bg-brand-hover transition duration-300">
        Go to the Calorie Deficit Calculator
      </a>
